Z||000002||Nashco||11-27-2001||08:14 PM||||Is there ANYBODY who's done a direct comparison (before and after swap) with coilovers using KYB or Konis?  I was planning on getting the Konis...sparing no expense...but I've decided I could spend the money a lot better to improve the performance of my car.  I would love the adjustability, but I'm a LONG ways from being able to put that kind of adjustability to use, so the adjustable coilover should be enough.<P>By the way (to keep things on topic <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">) I bought my front konis from shox.com and was VERY satisfied with everything.  They were courteous (even after I got the parts and had a question), shipped fast, and have the best prices by far.  There was a special when I ordered my konis as well, free shipping with all konis..so that was a bonus!<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phil||11-27-2001||08:39 PM||||To put coilovers on the rear using Koni's is VERY expensive, a regular coilover conversion  kit won't fit over Koni struts. Held Motor Sports make a special strut using a Koni cartridge but lotsa $$$||||reg||1
Z||000004||Old Lar||11-27-2001||09:06 PM||||Its hard to compare before and after with shocks.  I would not be replacing good shocks with new ones (expensive ones at that).  I'd be replacing bad shocks with new ones, and just about any new shock would be better than the old bad ones.||||reg||1
